xnu 0417: what is held of a scanout pool surface no program has goes without waiting for the next frame

0416 took away the largest holder of dead framebuffers (the released IOSurface views). Three more held a surface of the
framebuffer's scanout pool, and its 5 MiB slot, after every program had let it go (dev-notes/investigacion/anexos/
18-presentacion-y-dpu/01-retencion-de-frames.md, R2, R4, R5):

- A GPU buffer closed while the GPU still had work on it waits in its client's fLeft until reap(), and reap() ran only at
  that client's next GEM_SUBMIT. With the display off the window server submits nothing: the buffer, and the surface
  under it, stayed until "on", when CoreAnimation makes its new framebuffers before its first submission.
- The frame on the panel (fScanShow) of a backboardd that exited stayed until the new one presented and the MDP took
  that flip (removeClient() only took the client off the list); a swap recorded while the display was off (fPendHold)
  held its surface until "on", also when its window server had gone.
- directPresent()'s fLastSurf (3x only) was never let go when present() went the copy's way.

Now:
- SapphireGpu.cpp: the open clients are listed (msm_clients[], service_lock). GEM_NEW, GEM_CLOSE and the surface request
  reap first; a buffer of a surface left arms a 50 ms thread call that reaps every client until none is left; and
  sapphire_gpu_reap_all() reaps every client for the pool before it refuses a surface and for the census. clientClose()
  already freed everything after waiting for the GPU; a client also leaves the list in free(). What is freed is what
  the GPU is done with (its fence), as before.
- SweetSurface.cpp: a full pool reaps and looks once more before it gives the surface ordinary memory; the last user of
  a pool surface going (sweet_surface_put()) has the framebuffer look, from a thread call, for what it holds of it.
- SweetFramebuffer.cpp (surfacesGone()): a swap kept while off whose surface left the table is let go and its layer
  taken out of the pending record (nothing of it is shown at "on"); the frame on the panel of such a surface, with the
  display on and the queue empty, is shown again by the copy into a flip buffer, as scanoutStop() does: the picture
  stays and the surface goes when the MDP takes that flip (scanoutRetire()). The frames the MDP may still read
  (fScanLeave, the queue) keep their rules. present() lets go of fLastSurf with fLastVA when there are flip buffers
  (then directPresent() copied it, it was not scanned).

kern.sweet_fb_scanout_diag reaps and looks before it describes the pool, and says what each of these freed:
"0417: GPU buffers of surfaces freed at their fence …; pool full: a slot after reaping …; framebuffer: …".

Not measured on the phone yet: the protocol is in 18-presentacion-y-dpu/04-parches-0417-0418.md.
